"""Narration for the video on "Heavy Ion Collisions: The Big Picture, and the Big Questions"
(Busza, Rajagopal, van der Schee, arXiv:1802.04801). All drawings are made in chalk; no paper figures are used.

  python prep.py tts      -> audio/sNN.wav (sends the narration text to Microsoft's online TTS)
"""
import asyncio, os, subprocess, sys, wave

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
AUD = os.path.join(HERE, 'audio')

NARR = [
"What is the hottest liquid ever made on Earth? It is not in a furnace, or in a fusion reactor. It is made at particle colliders, when two heavy nuclei, like lead or gold, crash into each other at almost the speed of light. For a brief moment, they create a tiny droplet of quark gluon plasma, at a temperature of a few trillion degrees. This video follows a review article by Wit Busza, Krishna Rajagopal, and Wilke van der Schee. Its title is: Heavy Ion Collisions: The Big Picture, and the Big Questions.",
"Let us watch one collision, step by step. Because of relativity, each nucleus is flattened into a thin disc. A lead nucleus is about 14 femtometers wide, but at the Large Hadron Collider, its thickness is squeezed by a factor of about 2500. The two discs pass through each other, and they leave behind a region filled with energy. A very short time later, the time that light needs to travel one femtometer, the energy density there is still about 12 giga electron volts per cubic femtometer. That is about twenty times the energy density inside a typical hadron, such as a proton. So this matter cannot be made of separate protons and neutrons. It is made of their ingredients: quarks and gluons. In the end, a single collision can produce as many as thirty thousand particles.",
"Why do this? The first reason is the early universe. A few microseconds after the Big Bang, all of space was filled with this same kind of matter. It was too hot for protons or neutrons to exist. As the universe cooled, the plasma turned into the protons and neutrons that we are made of. Calculations on supercomputers, called lattice QCD, show that this change was not a sharp transition, like water freezing. It was a smooth crossover, at a temperature of about 150 mega electron volts. And a smooth crossover leaves no trace that telescopes could see today. So, to study big bang matter, we have to recreate it. Heavy ion collisions are little bangs.",
"Then came the big surprise. Physicists expected a gas of quarks and gluons, flying around almost freely. What they found was a liquid. How can you tell? When two nuclei collide off center, the overlap region is shaped like an almond. Now compare two possibilities. In a gas, the particles rarely meet. They fly off in random directions, and the final spray is the same in all directions. In a liquid, pressure matters. The pressure drops more steeply along the short side of the almond, so the liquid is pushed out faster that way. More particles come out along the short side. Hydrodynamics converts a shape in space into a pattern in momentum. And that pattern is exactly what the detectors see.",
"This pattern is called anisotropic flow. Physicists count the particles around the beam, as a function of the angle phi, and describe the pattern with numbers called v two, v three, and so on. V two measures the elliptical part. It grows as collisions become more off center. V three would be zero for perfectly smooth nuclei. It is not zero, because nuclei are lumpy: they are made of individual protons and neutrons. For these patterns to survive, the liquid must have a very small viscosity. The key number is the viscosity divided by the entropy density, eta over s. For quark gluon plasma, it is close to one over four pi. That is lower than for any other known liquid. It is nearly a perfect liquid.",
"So here is the life of the droplet. Within about one femtometer over c, the plasma forms, and starts to behave as a fluid. It expands, and it cools. Its own pressure drives it outward, at about half the speed of light. When the temperature falls to about 155 mega electron volts, the liquid turns into hadrons: pions, kaons, protons, and others. The mix of particle types is fixed around this point. This is called chemical freeze out. The hadrons keep bumping into each other for a little longer, until about 95 mega electron volts. After that, they fly freely to the detectors. Everything we know, we learn from these final particles.",
"Are the quarks really free inside the droplet? Heavy quarks give a test. An Upsilon is a bound pair of a bottom quark and its antiquark. It comes in several sizes. In the plasma, the surrounding color charges screen the force that holds the pair together. The small, tightly bound Upsilon can survive. The larger, loosely bound ones melt. And that is what the data show: the larger states are strongly suppressed in lead lead collisions. Charm quarks tell the other half of the story. At the Large Hadron Collider, about thirty charm pairs are made in one collision. A charm quark can wander through the plasma, and pair up with an anticharm quark from a different pair, making a new J psi. Quarks that wander independently are not confined inside hadrons.",
"There is a second, independent way to probe the liquid. Sometimes, two quarks or gluons collide very hard, right at the start. Each one flies out and becomes a narrow spray of particles, called a jet. But here, the jets must plow through the droplet. And they lose a lot of energy on the way: often 10 giga electron volts or more, over just a few femtometers. This is called jet quenching. Physicists measure it with a ratio called R sub A A: the number of jets seen, divided by the number expected without any plasma. For jets in head on collisions, it is well below one. For photons and Z bosons, which do not feel the strong force, it is one. The lost energy is not gone. It reappears as many soft particles at wide angles, like a wake behind a boat.",
"The review ends with big open questions. Here are four of them. One: how does the plasma form, and start to flow, in so short a time? Two: how small can a droplet be? Even collisions of single protons show some of the same flow patterns. Three: at very short distances, quarks and gluons interact only weakly. So how does a strongly coupled liquid emerge from them? Jets may one day serve as a microscope to see this. And four: what happens when the plasma has more quarks than antiquarks? The smooth crossover may turn into a sharp transition, at a critical point. Experiments at lower collision energies are searching for it.",
"Let us sum up. Heavy ion collisions recreate droplets of the matter that filled the universe in its first microseconds. Within about one femtometer over c, a quark gluon plasma forms. It is not a gas, but a liquid, with the smallest specific viscosity known. We see this in the flow patterns of the final particles. We see that the quarks are free in the melting of Upsilons. And we see how strongly the liquid interacts in the quenching of jets. How such a liquid emerges from the simple laws of quarks and gluons is still an open question. Thanks for watching.",
]


async def tts(voice='en-US-AvaNeural', rate='-6%'):
    import edge_tts
    os.makedirs(AUD, exist_ok=True)
    for i, t in enumerate(NARR, 1):
        mp3 = os.path.join(AUD, 's%02d.mp3' % i); wav = os.path.join(AUD, 's%02d.wav' % i)
        await edge_tts.Communicate(t, voice, rate=rate).save(mp3)
        subprocess.run(['ffmpeg', '-y', '-loglevel', 'error', '-i', mp3, '-ar', '22050', '-ac', '1', '-c:a', 'pcm_s16le', wav], check=True)
        with wave.open(wav) as w: print('s%02d: %.1f s' % (i, w.getnframes() / w.getframerate()), flush=True)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    if len(sys.argv) > 1 and sys.argv[1] == 'tts': asyncio.run(tts())
    else: print(__doc__)
